Authoritative cumulative snapshot of ref/linux-7.1.5: everything the
cooperative guest needs, as one diff against the released tree.

Generated against download/linux-7.1.5.tar.xz on 2026-08-08, and verified
by reverse-applying it to ref/linux-7.1.5 with no rejects.

Over the previous snapshot:

  * SMP rung S3: a second processor, online. "smp: Brought up 1 node,
    2 CPUs", verified on the M92p under Windows 10.

    An inert struct apic replaces reliance on nolapic, installed from
    parse_smp_cfg time rather than smp_prepare_cpus -- earlier is before
    apic_setup_apic_calls() has established the APIC static calls, and the
    guest stops a few lines after the early console. Setting
    smp_found_config makes init_apic_mappings() skip apic_disable(), so
    without a driver of our own the static calls stay on apic_physflat and
    every read and every send_IPI touches an APIC fixmap that was never
    mapped.

    co_colinux_smp_config() registers apics from 0, not 1.
    topology_register_boot_apic() calls topo_register_apic() directly and
    so never runs check_for_real_bsp(), leaving real_bsp_apic_id unset;
    starting at 1 made the first secondary the one to run that check, and
    it concluded this was a crash kernel and rejected the processor.

    IPIs are shared-memory pending bitmaps plus a doorbell: the sender sets
    the vector's bit in the target's set and crosses with KICK_VCPU, which
    only wakes the target -- a processor in the host's idle wait has
    nothing to re-enter it. Drained at the idle boundary inside
    irq_enter()/irq_exit(), and gated on a non-empty set: the pairing takes
    the is_idle_task() branch into tick_irq_enter() and suspends the RCU
    extended quiescent state, and running that on every idle with nothing
    to do killed the boot processor.

    The AP carries its TSS descriptor across the GDT switch. cpu_init()
    ends in load_fixmap_gdt(), which on a secondary is no longer skipped --
    the asm/desc.h guard is "co_colinux_guest && !co_colinux_own_idt" and
    the boot processor already set that flag. This CPU's gdt_page has no
    TSS descriptor at all, because set_tss_desc() lives only inside
    cpu_init_exception_handling(), which this path does not call, while
    linuxvm_state.tr stays 0x40. The switch reloads TR on every entry with
    no present or type check, so the first host interrupt afterwards ran
    ltr against sixteen zero bytes: #GP with CR3 already the guest's, RSP
    still the host's and TR still Windows' -- because the ltr is what
    failed. Every passage-page gate is IST1, so delivering it read the TSS
    through that stale TR, a Windows address unmapped here, and #PF and
    #DF repeated the identical read. Triple fault, no bugcheck, no dump.

    load_fixmap_gdt() never loads the read-only alias in a cooperative
    guest. The switch writes to the live GDT on every entry -- it clears
    the TSS busy bit before ltr, and ltr sets it again -- and through the
    PAGE_KERNEL_RO cpu-entry-area alias each store is a page fault taken
    inside the crossing.

    Also: apic ids are fabricated as CPU numbers in parse_topology because
    CPUID leaf 1 is not virtualised and reports the host core's id; the
    MP-table hooks are disabled, because parsing the host's table walked
    early_memremap over the read-only zero-page alias and WARNed, and WARN
    is ud2; ticks belong to vCPU 0 until each vCPU has a clock event device
    of its own, since one shared last_mono and a single evtdev on cpumask 0
    stop jiffies machine-wide the first time a secondary idles.

    The secondary runs Linux code, which means it needs Linux's exception
    handling. co_colinux_ap_adopt_traps() builds a hybrid IDT per vCPU --
    kernel gates 0-31 from idt_table, NMI and 32-255 re-pointed at THIS
    vCPU's passage stubs, harvested from the IDT live at that moment. The
    global idt_table cannot be loaded on a secondary: its external gates
    carry vCPU 0's stubs, and a stub is a crossing through its own passage
    page's saved state. Until this landed, every extable fixup, every WARN
    and every text_poke INT3 window on CPU 1 was a vector < 32 that stopped
    its lane dead -- measured as an rdmsr in perf_msr_probe, which the
    extable was waiting to fix up, and seen only as an RCU stall 21 seconds
    later. The TSS descriptor swap that goes with it is a cmpxchg16b,
    because the direct GDT is already live on a secondary and a crossing
    can re-enter between any two stores.

    csd_lock_wait() polls this processor's posted vectors. On real hardware
    that spin is bounded because the incoming CALL_FUNCTION IPI preempts
    it; nothing preempts kernel mode here, so two processors cross-calling
    each other -- a text_poke_sync against a TLB shootdown is enough --
    starved forever with no fault, no tick and no watchdog.

    Each vCPU has a clock event device of its own, and its own baseline
    against the host clock. One device on cpumask_of(0) left a secondary
    with no tick at all: nothing expired its timer wheel, so every msleep()
    that landed there slept forever.

    The cobd completion ring is claimed rather than walked. Two processors
    reach a drain boundary at once and both used to walk the same slots,
    ending one request twice -- blk_update_request on a request already
    ended and recycled, in userspace, seconds after /sbin/init. Each
    consumer now takes one index with a cmpxchg and owns that slot, so both
    drain in parallel on different entries. Teardown waits for the
    secondary lanes before stopping the block workers, since a secondary
    answers BLOCK_IO out of its own lane.

